Thanks.

00:05:43.610 --> 00:05:45.370
Yeah, that's great.

00:05:45.370 --> 00:05:47.620
And Oscar, I know we
chatted a little bit

00:05:47.620 --> 00:05:50.853
about some discussion
points that we wanted

00:05:50.853 --> 00:05:52.270
to bring up in the
beginning as we

00:05:52.270 --> 00:05:54.020
wait for questions to come in.

00:05:54.020 --> 00:05:56.650
But one thing I wanted
to ask you about

00:05:56.650 --> 00:06:01.990
is you mentioned theory of
constraints in visible work

00:06:01.990 --> 00:06:04.750
along with the negative
feedback loop in data work.

00:06:04.750 --> 00:06:07.600
And I was curious to
have you expand on that

00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:11.380
and share a little bit more
about what that means to you.

00:06:11.380 --> 00:06:16.000
Yeah, so I've always managed--

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:18.940
a bit of the background here
is, I've always found my--

00:06:18.940 --> 00:06:21.940
I've always thought of
myself, as a competent project

00:06:21.940 --> 00:06:24.280
manager of projects,
whereas it doesn't matter--

00:06:24.280 --> 00:06:28.160
I mean, I wouldn't say I'm the
best ever, but I'm pretty OK,

00:06:28.160 --> 00:06:28.660
I'd say.

00:06:28.660 --> 00:06:30.520
I get things delivered
on time, and usually

00:06:30.520 --> 00:06:34.630
within budget, and sometimes
very difficult conditions.

00:06:34.630 --> 00:06:37.390
And so, it really interests me
a lot about the management of

00:06:37.390 --> 00:06:38.200
projects.

00:06:38.200 --> 00:06:41.350
And I even wrote
a very short book

00:06:41.350 --> 00:06:43.060
that's free to
newsletter subscribers,

00:06:43.060 --> 00:06:44.920
but it can be free to
anyone here as well,

00:06:44.920 --> 00:06:46.570
about project
management fundamentals,

00:06:46.570 --> 00:06:48.640
specifically for data analysts.

00:06:48.640 --> 00:06:50.470
So I think about this
type of thing a lot.

00:06:50.470 --> 00:06:56.100
And I'm aware of-- you get
agile practices and a few others

00:06:56.100 --> 00:06:58.120
for managing projects.

00:06:58.120 --> 00:07:03.700
But I only very
recently picked up

00:07:03.700 --> 00:07:05.620
this book, The
Phoenix Project, which

00:07:05.620 --> 00:07:07.930
I'm sure probably many
people here have read

00:07:07.930 --> 00:07:09.160
or some have heard.

00:07:09.160 --> 00:07:10.660
I would highly
recommend reading it.

00:07:10.660 --> 00:07:14.690
It's really-- it's
interesting from a few ways.

00:07:14.690 --> 00:07:19.180
One in its delivery of teaching
you theoretical material

00:07:19.180 --> 00:07:21.138
through the use of a novel.

00:07:21.138 --> 00:07:23.180
I'd never experienced
something like that before.

00:07:23.180 --> 00:07:25.870
So that was just
interesting on its own.

00:07:25.870 --> 00:07:29.510
But the second was that it
introduced such teaching

00:07:29.510 --> 00:07:31.930
in this concept, the
theory of constraints,

00:07:31.930 --> 00:07:36.550
and how it relates to management
of delivery of projects.

00:07:36.550 --> 00:07:39.680
That book itself is based on
another book called The Goal.

00:07:39.680 --> 00:07:42.730
And The Goal was
written in the '80s by--

00:07:42.730 --> 00:07:44.965
I can't remember his
name exactly, but it's--

00:07:44.965 --> 00:07:47.050
his surname is
Goldratt, I think.

00:07:47.050 --> 00:07:57.620
And this-- so he gets talking
about the theory of constraints

00:07:57.620 --> 00:08:01.190
and what its implication
is for how you manage work,

00:08:01.190 --> 00:08:06.110
and how you assign
resources, and how you, yeah,

00:08:06.110 --> 00:08:08.760
prioritize things, basically.

00:08:08.760 --> 00:08:11.130
And why it's also interesting
is because out of it,

00:08:11.130 --> 00:08:15.460
out of all this, this idea
of visible work also emerges.

00:08:15.460 --> 00:08:18.540
So these things are all
quite tightly interwoven.

00:08:18.540 --> 00:08:21.000
And I've developed this--

00:08:21.000 --> 00:08:24.840
I had a bit of an epiphany while
reading The Goal, which is--

00:08:24.840 --> 00:08:28.980
and I think I need to read
both again to really dig in--

00:08:28.980 --> 00:08:31.080
but I had a bit of
an epiphany that I'm

00:08:31.080 --> 00:08:34.650
hoping others recognize, or
they've seen the solution to,

00:08:34.650 --> 00:08:37.530
or people just like, oh,
yeah, that is a thing,

00:08:37.530 --> 00:08:41.190
or am I just being
a bit crazy here?

00:08:41.190 --> 00:08:45.330
So if you are not familiar
with the theory of constraints,

00:08:45.330 --> 00:08:47.520
the very, very
basics of it is that

00:08:47.520 --> 00:08:49.890
in any system of
production, and that

00:08:49.890 --> 00:08:53.610
includes knowledge work, where
your work is being handed off

00:08:53.610 --> 00:08:57.730
between people, or in a
manufacturing setting,

00:08:57.730 --> 00:09:00.850
your car parts need to
go through some machine

00:09:00.850 --> 00:09:01.990
at some point.

00:09:01.990 --> 00:09:06.970
Somewhere in this line of
the series of work events,

00:09:06.970 --> 00:09:09.310
one or more of the
items, or one or more

00:09:09.310 --> 00:09:13.350
of the work sensors, as they
call it, is your bottleneck.

00:09:13.350 --> 00:09:15.120
Your bottleneck is
your constraints.

00:09:15.120 --> 00:09:16.440
And you need to--

00:09:16.440 --> 00:09:20.270
you'll get optimum performance
out of the whole system

00:09:20.270 --> 00:09:22.490
if you design around
the constraints.

00:09:22.490 --> 00:09:25.880
Most people design
around the full capacity

00:09:25.880 --> 00:09:28.970
of maximizing every single
work census capacity.

00:09:28.970 --> 00:09:30.870
And that causes huge
all sorts of issues.

00:09:30.870 --> 00:09:32.720
I'm not going to
recount the whole book.

00:09:32.720 --> 00:09:36.170
But the idea is that you
basically scale everything done

00:09:36.170 --> 00:09:41.290
and keep the constraints fully,
let's say, fully capacitated.

00:09:41.290 --> 00:09:45.050
And when I was reading
it, I thought, yeah,

00:09:45.050 --> 00:09:46.050
it makes a lot of sense.

00:09:46.050 --> 00:09:47.880
And a lot of what
they were saying I

00:09:47.880 --> 00:09:50.700
recognize in my own
stuff I've learned

00:09:50.700 --> 00:09:52.650
in a reverse-engineered
kind of way.

00:09:52.650 --> 00:09:54.450
Like, yeah, you don't
want to maximize

00:09:54.450 --> 00:09:57.880
everyone's capacity, and
queues, and workloads, and this.

00:09:57.880 --> 00:09:59.850
So it was really quite
mind-blowing, lots

00:09:59.850 --> 00:10:00.990
of what I was reading.

00:10:00.990 --> 00:10:03.270
But there's one thing that I--

00:10:03.270 --> 00:10:06.250
now, here's my
epiphany-- that the idea

00:10:06.250 --> 00:10:10.390
is that your constraint needs
to be able to keep pulling work

00:10:10.390 --> 00:10:11.498
as it's available.

00:10:11.498 --> 00:10:13.540
And that's the whole idea
of having visible work.

00:10:13.540 --> 00:10:15.370
You keep pulling work
as it's available.

00:10:15.370 --> 00:10:18.040
And that's work going
through the constraints

00:10:18.040 --> 00:10:19.660
goes to other work sensors.

00:10:19.660 --> 00:10:22.990
If there's any delay
at the constraint

00:10:22.990 --> 00:10:25.930
or any lowering of capacity
at the constraints,

00:10:25.930 --> 00:10:29.950
the problem is that you keep
building-- each of those delays

00:10:29.950 --> 00:10:31.360
becomes cumulative.

00:10:31.360 --> 00:10:33.010
And that's why small
delays, especially

00:10:33.010 --> 00:10:35.285
if your constraint's
somewhere near the front,

00:10:35.285 --> 00:10:38.410
or I don't remember
the exact positioning,

00:10:38.410 --> 00:10:40.750
but you get very
large delays later on.

00:10:40.750 --> 00:10:43.160
And that's why
you'll see, I mean,

00:10:43.160 --> 00:10:44.800
you can see it when
people are working,

00:10:44.800 --> 00:10:47.470
small issues up front
becomes someone else's

00:10:47.470 --> 00:10:49.900
massive nightmare
down the chain.

00:10:49.900 --> 00:10:51.910
And they can just never
catch up with work

00:10:51.910 --> 00:10:53.320
that was overwhelming.

00:10:53.320 --> 00:10:55.730
Because they're dealing with
all this upstream cumulative

00:10:55.730 --> 00:10:56.230
delays.

00:10:56.230 --> 00:11:01.670
And the cumulative delays are
super important to be aware of.

00:11:01.670 --> 00:11:05.480
But now, the solution,
I think, doesn't quite

00:11:05.480 --> 00:11:08.600
work for much of
data work, that just

00:11:08.600 --> 00:11:14.630
in maximizing the capacity
of your bottleneck.

00:11:14.630 --> 00:11:17.570
And I think, the reason
is, now, I'm not talking--

00:11:17.570 --> 00:11:20.390
so most of my data work has
been around, you could say,

00:11:20.390 --> 00:11:23.780
data analytical work,
not productionizing

00:11:23.780 --> 00:11:24.870
machine learning systems.

00:11:24.870 --> 00:11:27.950
I think productionizing that
is more akin to the DevOps

00:11:27.950 --> 00:11:31.160
environment, like in IT,
someone designing and developing

00:11:31.160 --> 00:11:34.720
something and then handing
over to someone else to deploy.

00:11:34.720 --> 00:11:37.310
That's more like how
everything else usually works.

00:11:37.310 --> 00:11:41.000
But in a lot of data work
that's very iterative,

00:11:41.000 --> 00:11:45.200
there's a huge problem
in that, most likely,

00:11:45.200 --> 00:11:52.950
your bottleneck becomes a
victim of its own delays.

00:11:52.950 --> 00:11:55.650
Because work often is not done.

00:11:55.650 --> 00:11:57.210
It's not just handed over.

00:11:57.210 --> 00:12:01.292
By necessity, you've got to hand
over an analysis to someone.

00:12:01.292 --> 00:12:02.500
But that's not the end of it.

00:12:02.500 --> 00:12:04.380
We all know it comes back again.

00:12:04.380 --> 00:12:09.600
And a lot of the techniques that
otherwise you might build in

00:12:09.600 --> 00:12:13.740
to reduce that rework--

00:12:13.740 --> 00:12:16.650
in a software setting, the
whole theory of contracts

00:12:16.650 --> 00:12:18.660
talks about having
feedback of information.

00:12:18.660 --> 00:12:20.130
But not of the work itself.

00:12:20.130 --> 00:12:22.500
The work itself isn't
supposed to come back.

00:12:22.500 --> 00:12:25.830
We deal with the work coming
back again because you can't--

00:12:25.830 --> 00:12:29.460
a lot of the work, you can't--
especially if it's exploratory,

00:12:29.460 --> 00:12:31.043
any kind of exploratory
data analysis,

00:12:31.043 --> 00:12:33.543
you're doing a research, and
you're not sure what the answer

00:12:33.543 --> 00:12:34.410
is going to be like.

00:12:34.410 --> 00:12:37.410
There's no way you
can specify upfront

00:12:37.410 --> 00:12:38.970
what the outputs should be.

00:12:38.970 --> 00:12:41.580
And no matter what
you did at the first--

00:12:41.580 --> 00:12:43.950
how good your
specification was, you

00:12:43.950 --> 00:12:47.490
will still get a reaction
from whoever is consuming it

00:12:47.490 --> 00:12:50.250
that they want more
information, or a feedback

00:12:50.250 --> 00:12:53.980
that once your bottleneck
has freed themselves,

00:12:53.980 --> 00:12:55.980
usually it's your analysts
and that of the work,

00:12:55.980 --> 00:12:57.002
it comes back.

00:12:57.002 --> 00:12:57.960
That's just the nature.

00:12:57.960 --> 00:12:59.880
The reason it comes
back is because you

00:12:59.880 --> 00:13:02.160
were successful in the
first place, I think.

00:13:02.160 --> 00:13:08.500
So now, the only
further bits I've

00:13:08.500 --> 00:13:10.630
gotten of how you might
be able to deal with this

00:13:10.630 --> 00:13:13.210
is when you need to tweak
the whole visible work

00:13:13.210 --> 00:13:15.040
and all of that system
a little bit is not

00:13:15.040 --> 00:13:18.330
to maximize your constraints.

00:13:18.330 --> 00:13:21.540
Your constraints also
need slack to take up

00:13:21.540 --> 00:13:23.870
the rework that's coming back.

00:13:23.870 --> 00:13:28.530
And the reworking of
work that comes back,

00:13:28.530 --> 00:13:31.940
you need to probably have some
variable system that you'll--

00:13:31.940 --> 00:13:34.220
I mean, it's going to
be very hard to get this

00:13:34.220 --> 00:13:35.120
to an exact science.

00:13:35.120 --> 00:13:37.910
But it will be a function
of how much have you already

00:13:37.910 --> 00:13:39.440
pushed out?

00:13:39.440 --> 00:13:42.040
So the more that you pushed
out, the more variability

00:13:42.040 --> 00:13:45.670
you need to leave
in your capacity

00:13:45.670 --> 00:13:48.460
until you show that this is
not going to come back again.

00:13:48.460 --> 00:13:50.860
You'll need some way to
know that this work ticket

00:13:50.860 --> 00:13:51.550
item is done.

00:13:51.550 --> 00:13:52.370
The project's done.

00:13:52.370 --> 00:13:54.610
The investment's
been made, whatever,

00:13:54.610 --> 00:13:57.260
and you're not going to
get more coming back.

00:13:57.260 --> 00:14:01.690
So that was quite
a big aha moment

00:14:01.690 --> 00:14:02.940
that I had a little while ago.

00:14:02.940 --> 00:14:05.107
Because I was thinking, how
could we implement this?

00:14:05.107 --> 00:14:07.960
And I just saw it just wouldn't
work, it's like, let's say,

00:14:07.960 --> 00:14:08.897
true form.

00:14:08.897 --> 00:14:09.730
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:14:09.730 --> 00:14:12.610
It's interesting you just
said that exact part there.

00:14:12.610 --> 00:14:15.010
Because I was just reading
that part in the Making

00:14:15.010 --> 00:14:16.823
Work Visible book.

00:14:16.823 --> 00:14:18.740
It's taking me a little
bit to get through it.

00:14:18.740 --> 00:14:21.677
But we just talked
about that part.

00:14:21.677 --> 00:14:22.510
OSCAR BARUFFA: Yeah.

00:14:22.510 --> 00:14:24.190
RACHAEL DEMPSEY: I
see, Marlene, you

00:14:24.190 --> 00:14:26.740
had a follow-up
question to what Oscar

00:14:26.740 --> 00:14:28.300
is talking about right now.

00:14:28.300 --> 00:14:30.830
Do you want to jump in?

00:14:30.830 --> 00:14:31.330
Hi, Oscar.

00:14:31.330 --> 00:14:33.510
Thanks for the
talk this morning--

00:14:33.510 --> 00:14:35.800
well, it's morning where I'm at.

00:14:35.800 --> 00:14:37.770
I'm just curious.

00:14:37.770 --> 00:14:39.660
You're talking
about decisions that

00:14:39.660 --> 00:14:42.930
get compounded down
the line, especially

00:14:42.930 --> 00:14:44.100
in production systems.

00:14:44.100 --> 00:14:46.510
I'm just wondering,
in your experience,

00:14:46.510 --> 00:14:50.530
how much of those constraints
have happened from managers,

00:14:50.530 --> 00:14:55.440
supervisors, c-suite, anybody
above you on the totem pole

00:14:55.440 --> 00:15:00.600
versus maybe lateral
to you or below.

00:15:00.600 --> 00:15:02.940
Roughly what percentage do
you think you've seen that?

00:15:07.930 --> 00:15:10.328
Good question.

00:15:10.328 --> 00:15:10.870
I don't know.

00:15:10.870 --> 00:15:11.890
It would be hard.

00:15:11.890 --> 00:15:18.580
I think it's maybe more those
who are dependent on the output

00:15:18.580 --> 00:15:22.150
to move forward at all are
the ones who, I would say,

00:15:22.150 --> 00:15:24.730
maybe more excluding
the c-suite,

00:15:24.730 --> 00:15:26.740
or the executive decision-maker.

00:15:26.740 --> 00:15:29.920
Because my expense
is, in a way, they

00:15:29.920 --> 00:15:32.230
don't seem to get impacted
at all other than still

00:15:32.230 --> 00:15:35.470
just moving forward
with actual information.

00:15:35.470 --> 00:15:37.060
So there's usually maybe--

00:15:37.060 --> 00:15:40.090
a lot of the work we do
is often in, as well,

00:15:40.090 --> 00:15:42.310
in publishing some
kind of documents

00:15:42.310 --> 00:15:45.550
or a report of some kind.

00:15:45.550 --> 00:15:47.680
It's that person, that
last one, maybe just

00:15:47.680 --> 00:15:49.820
before it gets to the
c-suite, like let's say

00:15:49.820 --> 00:15:51.403
in the theory of
constraints language,

00:15:51.403 --> 00:15:53.560
and that that's where
it leaves the workshop,

00:15:53.560 --> 00:15:56.480
at that technical
person's point of view.

00:15:56.480 --> 00:16:00.190
So I'd say it's, for me, it's I
would maybe classify it as 100%

00:16:00.190 --> 00:16:02.110
laterally.

00:16:02.110 --> 00:16:04.070
Above that from the decision--

00:16:04.070 --> 00:16:05.500
they need to go forward anyway.

00:16:05.500 --> 00:16:08.987
And that's also where it starts
becoming important to manage

00:16:08.987 --> 00:16:09.820
these things better.

00:16:09.820 --> 00:16:12.730
Because you don't want
to become irrelevant

00:16:12.730 --> 00:16:16.820
work center service provider
to the organization.

00:16:16.820 --> 00:16:18.820
If your stuff is just
never ready or good enough

00:16:18.820 --> 00:16:20.620
because of your
delays or whatever,

00:16:20.620 --> 00:16:22.020
people are going to go ahead.

00:16:22.020 --> 00:16:24.460
They were going ahead before
your data team was around,

00:16:24.460 --> 00:16:27.610
and they're going to keep
going ahead without you.

00:16:27.610 --> 00:16:30.250
Maybe not for the better of
the organization, but yeah,

00:16:30.250 --> 00:16:33.850
I'd say it's 100% laterally.

00:16:33.850 --> 00:16:37.310
That's for me, at least,
where my focus has been.

00:16:37.310 --> 00:16:38.030
Yeah, great.

00:16:38.030 --> 00:16:38.847
Thank you.

00:16:38.847 --> 00:16:40.660
OSCAR BARUFFA: Yeah.

00:16:40.660 --> 00:16:44.710
I see that there was a
question in Slido that was,

00:16:44.710 --> 00:16:49.537
what compelled you to
start the Big Book of R?

00:16:49.537 --> 00:16:50.620
Yeah, that's a good one.

00:16:50.620 --> 00:16:56.830
So I love talking about the Big
Book of R. So that was about,

00:16:56.830 --> 00:17:00.900
yes, it was August 2020
that I launched it.

00:17:00.900 --> 00:17:03.780
And a little bit
before that, I've

00:17:03.780 --> 00:17:06.339
just been bookmarking
lots and lots of books,

00:17:06.339 --> 00:17:10.010
especially then, well, I
mean, until just recently,

00:17:10.010 --> 00:17:11.650
I was on Twitter a lot.

00:17:11.650 --> 00:17:14.744
And then I was all
the time on Twitter.

00:17:14.744 --> 00:17:16.619
And people were sharing
their books and that.

00:17:16.619 --> 00:17:18.579
And I just started
bookmarking them.

00:17:18.579 --> 00:17:21.510
And I remember at
once seeing my set,

00:17:21.510 --> 00:17:25.410
my bookmarks folder had
80 of these R books.

00:17:25.410 --> 00:17:27.960
And I'd not seen a
collection of such.

00:17:27.960 --> 00:17:31.770
And I'd just put out
a call basically.

00:17:31.770 --> 00:17:33.480
I said, hey, anyone
else is collecting?

00:17:33.480 --> 00:17:34.710
How many do you have?

00:17:34.710 --> 00:17:36.450
I was just trying to compare.

00:17:36.450 --> 00:17:40.950
Am I-- and most people
coming back with 5, 10, 25.

00:17:40.950 --> 00:17:42.960
I thought, wow,
actually, I've somehow

00:17:42.960 --> 00:17:46.080
stumbled upon this
massive repository.

00:17:46.080 --> 00:17:48.540
And then also, it was
one of those things

00:17:48.540 --> 00:17:53.070
I thought, oh, maybe I could
just quickly put this together.

00:17:53.070 --> 00:17:54.840
Because I'd also
just actually written

00:17:54.840 --> 00:17:58.320
a book called Twitter
For R Programmers,

00:17:58.320 --> 00:18:02.280
which was a collaborative
effort with Veerle van Son.

00:18:02.280 --> 00:18:05.610
And so, I just had this-- it was
my first time using R Markdown,

00:18:05.610 --> 00:18:08.250
and I quite liked
the thought of being

00:18:08.250 --> 00:18:11.113
able to make a
book really easily.

00:18:11.113 --> 00:18:12.780
So then these things
just came together.

00:18:12.780 --> 00:18:15.760
I saw, oh, I've got this massive
thing, this great repository,

00:18:15.760 --> 00:18:19.330
and this great collection.

00:18:19.330 --> 00:18:22.600
So that was the start of
it, it's at around 80 books.

00:18:22.600 --> 00:18:28.330
And, yeah, it's been
really fun project.

00:18:28.330 --> 00:18:30.490
Thank you for that
contribution to the community

00:18:30.490 --> 00:18:30.990
too.

00:18:30.990 --> 00:18:34.990
I know there's so many people
who have used that resource.

00:18:34.990 --> 00:18:37.110
There is actually a
way you can help me,

00:18:37.110 --> 00:18:39.690
actually, everyone, if you want.

00:18:39.690 --> 00:18:45.330
There's a-- I'm applying
for a R Consortium grant

00:18:45.330 --> 00:18:48.840
to basically upgrade
the Big Book of R.

00:18:48.840 --> 00:18:52.710
And so, I'm collecting--
basically, I don't know,

00:18:52.710 --> 00:18:55.890
it's a 2-minute survey where
you can just add your name

00:18:55.890 --> 00:18:57.990
and if you think this
is a good idea or not.

00:18:57.990 --> 00:19:00.250
It's part of the submission.

00:19:00.250 --> 00:19:02.680
So I think I've got
about 110 responses.

00:19:02.680 --> 00:19:05.580
So if I can get 150,
then it can be really,

00:19:05.580 --> 00:19:08.650
wow, everyone really thinks
this is a great resource.

00:19:08.650 --> 00:19:10.620
So I'll share it in
the chat just now.

00:19:10.620 --> 00:19:12.785
I just put it in the chat too.

00:19:12.785 --> 00:19:13.410
But make sure--

00:19:13.410 --> 00:19:14.790
OSCAR BARUFFA: Ah, yeah, you
did. you are ahead of me.

00:19:14.790 --> 00:19:15.457
Thanks, Rachael.

00:19:15.457 --> 00:19:16.890
Yeah, fantastic.

00:19:16.890 --> 00:19:20.665
That would be great to get a
few more statements of support.

00:19:20.665 --> 00:19:21.165
Thanks.

00:19:21.165 --> 00:19:21.510
RACHAEL DEMPSEY: Absolutely.

00:19:21.510 --> 00:19:22.440
Yeah.

00:19:22.440 --> 00:19:24.090
RACHAEL DEMPSEY: Ben, I see
you had asked a question

00:19:24.090 --> 00:19:25.200
in the chat a bit earlier.

00:19:25.200 --> 00:19:27.220
Do you want to jump in?

00:19:27.220 --> 00:19:29.140
Yeah, Oscar,
first of all, thanks

00:19:29.140 --> 00:19:31.297
for all the contributions
to open source,

00:19:31.297 --> 00:19:32.630
especially in the R environment.

00:19:32.630 --> 00:19:37.180
But what benefits have you
seen from those contributions

00:19:37.180 --> 00:19:40.290
to your career overall?

00:19:40.290 --> 00:19:41.385
Wow, a good question.

00:19:44.270 --> 00:19:45.860
Yeah, I'd say quite a few.

00:19:45.860 --> 00:19:51.000
One is just that it's--

00:19:51.000 --> 00:19:51.890
yeah.

00:19:51.890 --> 00:19:55.395
How do I put this?

00:19:55.395 --> 00:19:58.440
There's a certain-- this
isn't the right word,

00:19:58.440 --> 00:20:00.870
but certain level of
confidence that you

00:20:00.870 --> 00:20:03.120
get that it's like, wow, I'm
doing something that gets

00:20:03.120 --> 00:20:06.030
validated quite quickly.

00:20:06.030 --> 00:20:08.220
A lot of our work,
a lot of my work

00:20:08.220 --> 00:20:11.695
anyway, there's so much stuff
that I really like doing,

00:20:11.695 --> 00:20:12.570
and it's really good.

00:20:12.570 --> 00:20:15.600
But who's going to know that
it's good or bad other than me?

00:20:15.600 --> 00:20:16.950
It's really difficult know.

00:20:16.950 --> 00:20:19.230
My managers are non-technical.

00:20:19.230 --> 00:20:23.670
If I make something that's
a really terrible back-end

00:20:23.670 --> 00:20:25.890
and everything's
all jangly together,

00:20:25.890 --> 00:20:29.490
or it's a super, super great
system, it's hard for them

00:20:29.490 --> 00:20:31.020
to do that.

00:20:31.020 --> 00:20:34.500
So this is one way
that's at least it's

00:20:34.500 --> 00:20:36.840
great to get that kind
of validation externally,

00:20:36.840 --> 00:20:40.020
that, yeah, I apply the
same kind of, let's say,

00:20:40.020 --> 00:20:44.200
level of effort and
enjoyment in both elements.

00:20:44.200 --> 00:20:45.510
So at least this is one--

00:20:45.510 --> 00:20:49.180
a sample of my work that
gets a lot of thumbs up.

00:20:49.180 --> 00:20:50.580
So that's really great.

00:20:50.580 --> 00:20:55.800
It's also been quite
useful in getting

00:20:55.800 --> 00:20:57.750
a good overview
of the R ecosystem

00:20:57.750 --> 00:20:59.610
and what people
are using it for.

00:20:59.610 --> 00:21:02.767
And for example,
I noticed if you

00:21:02.767 --> 00:21:04.350
want to do stats or
something, there's

00:21:04.350 --> 00:21:06.840
tons of books, lots of them.

00:21:06.840 --> 00:21:09.720
If you want to do geographic
information stuff,

00:21:09.720 --> 00:21:11.890
there's quite a
few books as well.

00:21:11.890 --> 00:21:14.430
I work a lot with Excel
in my daily work as well,

00:21:14.430 --> 00:21:15.540
lots, and lots, and lots.

00:21:15.540 --> 00:21:17.130
And there's Excel packages.

00:21:17.130 --> 00:21:19.980
No books about
working with Excel.

00:21:19.980 --> 00:21:22.710
And so, when I was doing
quite a big project--

00:21:22.710 --> 00:21:24.930
and we do interact
quite a lot with it.

00:21:24.930 --> 00:21:26.940
But I always knew
that at some point

00:21:26.940 --> 00:21:29.850
we would be limited
with what some of what R

00:21:29.850 --> 00:21:30.870
could do with Excel.

00:21:30.870 --> 00:21:32.560
And we did hit those limits.

00:21:32.560 --> 00:21:34.200
But I was not surprised
by them at all.

00:21:34.200 --> 00:21:35.160
That's a small thing.

00:21:35.160 --> 00:21:39.420
But had I not already had this
big overview of everything that

00:21:39.420 --> 00:21:45.120
goes on in R, I may have walked
a bit more blindly into that.

00:21:45.120 --> 00:21:47.620
And also, meeting people
has been really fantastic.

00:21:47.620 --> 00:21:50.490
So often people tag
me on stuff and then

00:21:50.490 --> 00:21:51.930
I start recognizing authors.

00:21:51.930 --> 00:21:57.930
Or I've been asked to review
some book proposals as well.

00:21:57.930 --> 00:22:00.360
So you'll get, especially
from CRC Press,

00:22:00.360 --> 00:22:03.000
they do quite a lot of R books.

00:22:03.000 --> 00:22:06.820
Publications, I've been asked
to look at some of those.

00:22:06.820 --> 00:22:10.530
It's quite nice to see what
some people are working on way

00:22:10.530 --> 00:22:12.750
before it's released.

00:22:12.750 --> 00:22:15.150
Yeah, so it's been
really fun all around.

00:22:15.150 --> 00:22:17.100
And I must say, that
it wasn't something

00:22:17.100 --> 00:22:18.392
that would happen at the start.

00:22:18.392 --> 00:22:20.410
I just thought, that
would be really cool.

00:22:20.410 --> 00:22:23.494
But it's been great fun.

00:22:23.494 --> 00:22:24.220
Awesome.

00:22:24.220 --> 00:22:27.370
I see there was an anonymous
question that I just

00:22:27.370 --> 00:22:28.660
want to make sure I covered.

00:22:28.660 --> 00:22:31.420
Somebody asked if there
was a grant program

00:22:31.420 --> 00:22:34.713
to assist students who wish
to attend Posit Conference?

00:22:34.713 --> 00:22:36.130
And I just wanted
to let everybody

00:22:36.130 --> 00:22:38.110
know that Hannah
shared in the chat

00:22:38.110 --> 00:22:41.410
that we do have an Opportunity
Scholars Program as well.

00:22:41.410 --> 00:22:44.110
And she put the link in there.

00:22:44.110 --> 00:22:46.220
But, Jonathan, I see you
have your hand raised.

00:22:46.220 --> 00:22:49.270
Do you want to jump in next?

00:22:49.270 --> 00:22:50.360
Yeah, absolutely.

00:22:50.360 --> 00:22:53.440
And, Oscar, thank you very
much for co-hosting today.

00:22:53.440 --> 00:22:55.780
Rachael, thanks for putting
this together, as always.

00:22:55.780 --> 00:23:01.330
So one thing I felt that I
could add to the conversation.

00:23:01.330 --> 00:23:03.550
I work in risk underwriting
for the subprime.

00:23:03.550 --> 00:23:05.342
I've been doing it for
about six years now.

00:23:05.342 --> 00:23:07.710
And it's a very flat
organization, FlexShopper.

00:23:07.710 --> 00:23:10.710
And one thing I've
learned within the theory

00:23:10.710 --> 00:23:12.450
of constraints,
applying it here,

00:23:12.450 --> 00:23:16.560
is that priorities tend
to shift very rapidly.

00:23:16.560 --> 00:23:20.160
And it really causes a
lot of context-switching

00:23:20.160 --> 00:23:21.150
amongst the team.

00:23:21.150 --> 00:23:28.230
And one way that I've introduced
to help to control that is--

00:23:28.230 --> 00:23:30.780
even though it's not ideal, and
hopefully maybe somebody else

00:23:30.780 --> 00:23:32.488
might be able to chime
in on this later--

00:23:32.488 --> 00:23:36.850
is just having them create
ticket stubs for everything

00:23:36.850 --> 00:23:37.350
they get.

00:23:37.350 --> 00:23:42.150
So in that effect, if
they get a-- if they

00:23:42.150 --> 00:23:45.180
get a new priority saying,
hey, get on this now, just

00:23:45.180 --> 00:23:46.950
put a ticket stub
in, have it there.

00:23:46.950 --> 00:23:49.450
So that way, when we're done,
we can go back and figure out,

00:23:49.450 --> 00:23:50.790
all right, well, what
are we doing over here?

00:23:50.790 --> 00:23:52.680
Oh, we were doing this,
that, and the other thing.

00:23:52.680 --> 00:23:53.260
Here we go.

00:23:53.260 --> 00:23:55.680
And it doesn't eliminate
the context-switching,

00:23:55.680 --> 00:23:57.180
but it definitely
helps to reduce it

00:23:57.180 --> 00:23:59.670
to the effect of
you can almost pick

00:23:59.670 --> 00:24:00.900
up right where you left off.

00:24:00.900 --> 00:24:03.750
And it's helped to bring up
the efficiency by around,

00:24:03.750 --> 00:24:08.760
I would say, 20% in terms
of turning tasks around.

00:24:08.760 --> 00:24:09.510
Yeah, no, great.

00:24:09.510 --> 00:24:12.810
Yeah, and the
context-switching is all--

00:24:12.810 --> 00:24:13.720
I've felt it myself.

00:24:13.720 --> 00:24:15.390
And I've seen it
with others as well.

00:24:15.390 --> 00:24:17.930
If you can reduce
context-switching outputs,

00:24:17.930 --> 00:24:20.022
it's massively--

00:24:20.022 --> 00:24:20.730
it's much better.

00:24:20.730 --> 00:24:23.520
But also, people's
job satisfaction

00:24:23.520 --> 00:24:25.680
is way higher, and
including my own.

00:24:25.680 --> 00:24:28.800
But it's getting harder and
harder to not context-switch.

00:24:28.800 --> 00:24:31.620
But I have noticed, even
for myself, that switching,

00:24:31.620 --> 00:24:33.632
even if it doesn't
get your work up--

00:24:33.632 --> 00:24:34.590
let me put it this way.

00:24:34.590 --> 00:24:37.380
Even if it didn't
improve productivity,

00:24:37.380 --> 00:24:39.750
it improves work satisfaction.

00:24:39.750 --> 00:24:43.680
It's a huge retention gain.

00:24:43.680 --> 00:24:48.340
But, yeah, that's great, and
especially on the tickets.

00:24:48.340 --> 00:24:49.230
That's a good one.

00:24:49.230 --> 00:24:50.440
Thanks, Jonathan.

00:24:50.440 --> 00:24:52.050
Yeah, thank you.

00:24:52.050 --> 00:24:54.047
Ahnas, I see you have
your hand raised too.

00:24:54.047 --> 00:24:54.880
You want to jump in?

00:24:54.880 --> 00:24:55.660
Yes.

00:24:55.660 --> 00:24:56.160
Thank you.

00:24:56.160 --> 00:24:57.780
Thank you, Rachael.

00:24:57.780 --> 00:25:01.740
So I'm going to piggyback off
Jonathan's question, Oscar.

00:25:01.740 --> 00:25:08.460
So I like how you describe
the difference between how

00:25:08.460 --> 00:25:11.670
the theory of constraints
affects it broadly,

00:25:11.670 --> 00:25:14.680
and then how it affects us
specifically in data science.

00:25:14.680 --> 00:25:18.630
So what are other
methods that you've

00:25:18.630 --> 00:25:22.740
tried to help reduce that piece
where we're repeating analysis.

00:25:22.740 --> 00:25:25.890
And it kind of left me
on a cliffhanger there.

00:25:25.890 --> 00:25:29.190
I'm like, what are some
things that you've experienced

00:25:29.190 --> 00:25:30.930
and things that you'd recommend?

00:25:30.930 --> 00:25:33.990
And then, I have a second
question, if I can.

00:25:33.990 --> 00:25:37.890
Going back to the Big
Book Of R, I sometimes

00:25:37.890 --> 00:25:40.110
feel like there's things
that I want to share,

00:25:40.110 --> 00:25:43.530
but I feel like, because
of the nature of my work,

00:25:43.530 --> 00:25:47.310
I have to hide what I'm doing--

00:25:47.310 --> 00:25:50.130
not hide, but there's
company confidentiality,

00:25:50.130 --> 00:25:52.978
and I want to obscure
that, then share.

00:25:52.978 --> 00:25:54.270
And that's just a tricky piece.

00:25:54.270 --> 00:25:57.210
And have you had to
deal with that before?

00:25:57.210 --> 00:26:00.300
Has anyone here had to deal
with that before, of wanting

00:26:00.300 --> 00:26:04.110
to share things they've done
in R, but at the same time,

00:26:04.110 --> 00:26:07.520
they're concerned about
their company's data--

00:26:07.520 --> 00:26:10.115
not the company's data,
but just that process?

00:26:14.220 --> 00:26:14.720
All right.

00:26:14.720 --> 00:26:18.170
So let me start with
on the repeat analysis.

00:26:18.170 --> 00:26:22.580
I think for me, it's not to
eliminate the repeats analysis.

00:26:22.580 --> 00:26:26.550
I think that repeat
analysis is precisely

00:26:26.550 --> 00:26:30.400
the valuable bits of the work.

00:26:30.400 --> 00:26:34.120
If you're with me, like getting
that, especially when there's

00:26:34.120 --> 00:26:37.750
some kind of domain expert, that
back and forth to get something

00:26:37.750 --> 00:26:40.270
out there is really valuable.

00:26:40.270 --> 00:26:42.220
And it's, actually,
that back and forth

00:26:42.220 --> 00:26:47.200
that I want to enable and
protect that both parties end

00:26:47.200 --> 00:26:48.130
up quite satisfied.

00:26:48.130 --> 00:26:50.870
Not to-- I mean, there's
a difference between,

00:26:50.870 --> 00:26:54.820
let's say, asking
someone to redo work

00:26:54.820 --> 00:26:57.670
because the communication was
faulty in the first place.

00:26:57.670 --> 00:26:59.920
That's a different problem.

00:26:59.920 --> 00:27:02.560
That's just getting the
two working together

00:27:02.560 --> 00:27:04.180
well, understanding
the definitions,

00:27:04.180 --> 00:27:05.650
or being clear on the output.

00:27:05.650 --> 00:27:07.150
But when things are
going very well,

00:27:07.150 --> 00:27:10.720
that back and forth and repeat
I quite like to keep going.

00:27:10.720 --> 00:27:14.650
Because then, for
every iteration,

00:27:14.650 --> 00:27:16.757
there's more value
being extracted out

00:27:16.757 --> 00:27:17.590
of the data we have.

00:27:17.590 --> 00:27:18.980
So I go, yeah, cool,
now can we look at this?

00:27:18.980 --> 00:27:20.000
Now can we look at that?

00:27:20.000 --> 00:27:20.600
What about this?

00:27:20.600 --> 00:27:21.267
What about that?

00:27:21.267 --> 00:27:24.190
I really, yeah, so I'll
try and encourage that.

00:27:24.190 --> 00:27:28.860
But it's how to
still let that happen

00:27:28.860 --> 00:27:34.893
is by protecting the time of
the person doing that work,

00:27:34.893 --> 00:27:37.060
that they don't feel like
they have to juggle and do

00:27:37.060 --> 00:27:37.510
the switching.

00:27:37.510 --> 00:27:39.010
Because then they
don't want to also

00:27:39.010 --> 00:27:41.500
get into this back and forth,
and can we look at this,

00:27:41.500 --> 00:27:42.530
can we look at that?

00:27:42.530 --> 00:27:44.110
So it's more about--

00:27:44.110 --> 00:27:46.550
I sort of see it as
is the stream of time

00:27:46.550 --> 00:27:47.800
that you're trying to protect.

00:27:47.800 --> 00:27:49.780
And as long as it's
needed, it's got

00:27:49.780 --> 00:27:52.867
to be insulated until
that's closed off.

00:27:52.867 --> 00:27:54.450
At some point, it
will come to an end.

00:27:54.450 --> 00:27:56.990
So yeah.

00:27:56.990 --> 00:28:01.670
So it's flipping on its
head a little bit there.

00:28:01.670 --> 00:28:02.960
On sharing work, yeah.

00:28:02.960 --> 00:28:04.830
So I can tell you what I do.

00:28:04.830 --> 00:28:08.360
I just don't share the stuff
that even I really want to, I

00:28:08.360 --> 00:28:08.920
just don't.

00:28:08.920 --> 00:28:10.910
I haven't found a
very good way for me

00:28:10.910 --> 00:28:14.000
to be able to show a
lot of what we're doing.

00:28:14.000 --> 00:28:19.760
But then, I zoom out a
little bit into other things

00:28:19.760 --> 00:28:20.960
that I find interesting.

00:28:20.960 --> 00:28:23.810
For example, I want to talk
about some of the projects

00:28:23.810 --> 00:28:24.800
we do.

00:28:24.800 --> 00:28:27.830
But it's a bit difficult. I
mean, I think at some point,

00:28:27.830 --> 00:28:29.900
I be may be able
to do it more when

00:28:29.900 --> 00:28:31.638
I've got more time to do it.

00:28:31.638 --> 00:28:32.930
But I'll zoom out a little bit.

00:28:32.930 --> 00:28:35.000
And what else do I
think is interesting,

00:28:35.000 --> 00:28:36.140
like project management?

00:28:36.140 --> 00:28:37.307
How do you manage your team?

00:28:40.772 --> 00:28:42.650
I'm trying to think
of some other things.

00:28:42.650 --> 00:28:46.070
I was quite interested in
some of these certifications

00:28:46.070 --> 00:28:49.290
that you see out there
that I've seen many,

00:28:49.290 --> 00:28:50.930
many new data
analysts are getting.

00:28:50.930 --> 00:28:52.430
And I wanted to--

00:28:52.430 --> 00:28:55.440
so like the Google data
analytics one, for example.

00:28:55.440 --> 00:28:57.650
I was super interested
in assessing

00:28:57.650 --> 00:29:01.300
what do I think about the
content as a hiring manager?

00:29:01.300 --> 00:29:03.610
Now, that's not
NDA stuff for us.

00:29:03.610 --> 00:29:04.180
It's nothing.

00:29:04.180 --> 00:29:05.485
So I went and did it.

00:29:05.485 --> 00:29:06.610
And it's still interesting.

00:29:06.610 --> 00:29:07.818
And then, that I wrote about.

00:29:07.818 --> 00:29:09.610
So it still scratches
that itch of being

00:29:09.610 --> 00:29:13.130
able to share information, not
always the exact information

00:29:13.130 --> 00:29:13.630
I want.

00:29:13.630 --> 00:29:16.900
What I have seen someone
else do is they took,

00:29:16.900 --> 00:29:18.580
for something they
worked on with us,

00:29:18.580 --> 00:29:23.077
they did a demo on their
own toy project to test it.

00:29:23.077 --> 00:29:25.410
And that's how they learned
before they came and did it.

00:29:25.410 --> 00:29:28.510
And then, that's
how they pitched us.

00:29:28.510 --> 00:29:31.660
No, we'd really like to do this
type of thing on your project

00:29:31.660 --> 00:29:33.610
or on this project
we're working on.

00:29:33.610 --> 00:29:36.130
But they'd just done
their demo of it

00:29:36.130 --> 00:29:38.740
publicly on a toy data set.

00:29:38.740 --> 00:29:42.040
So they still also got that
kind of scratching that itch

00:29:42.040 --> 00:29:44.020
while being able to
share it a little bit.

00:29:44.020 --> 00:29:44.650
But, yeah.

00:29:44.650 --> 00:29:47.020
But I'm keen on
if anyone else has

00:29:47.020 --> 00:29:54.030
been able to come up with some
cool NDA protection backend

00:29:54.030 --> 00:29:56.610
whatever.

00:29:56.610 --> 00:29:59.380
What would you say?

00:29:59.380 --> 00:30:00.810
I can't think of the word now.

00:30:00.810 --> 00:30:03.120
I see, Marlene,
you had some ideas

00:30:03.120 --> 00:30:04.710
of what you're working on.

00:30:07.320 --> 00:30:07.910
Say what?

00:30:07.910 --> 00:30:09.410
RACHAEL DEMPSEY: I
saw you said, you

00:30:09.410 --> 00:30:11.840
had some ideas of
how you were sharing

00:30:11.840 --> 00:30:14.550
this public-facing portfolio.

00:30:14.550 --> 00:30:17.000
So I was curious if you
wanted to share maybe

00:30:17.000 --> 00:30:19.250
how you were doing that?

00:30:19.250 --> 00:30:21.350
Oh, sure.

00:30:21.350 --> 00:30:25.640
So for those of you who don't
know, I work in agriculture.

00:30:25.640 --> 00:30:28.610
And some of the projects we
work on here, for example,

00:30:28.610 --> 00:30:30.110
are food safety-related,
where we're

00:30:30.110 --> 00:30:33.840
showing lab tests of products.

00:30:33.840 --> 00:30:36.770
So a piece of lettuce
that gets sent to a lab

00:30:36.770 --> 00:30:40.760
and then it gets homogenized and
tested for foodborne pathogens,

00:30:40.760 --> 00:30:43.820
like E coli.

00:30:43.820 --> 00:30:49.355
And then, we'll show those on
a map positive or negative.

00:30:49.355 --> 00:30:51.230
By the way, when you
see a positive on a map,

00:30:51.230 --> 00:30:52.522
doesn't mean anything terrible.

00:30:52.522 --> 00:30:55.070
It just means that something
lived there, an organism.

00:30:55.070 --> 00:30:58.310
But anyway, my point is
that maps can very easily

00:30:58.310 --> 00:30:59.550
be misconstrued.

00:30:59.550 --> 00:31:02.150
So I did a mock-up for this.

00:31:02.150 --> 00:31:06.200
It's right now
nationally blowing up

00:31:06.200 --> 00:31:08.690
this food safety data-sharing
project that my trade

00:31:08.690 --> 00:31:10.760
organization put together.

00:31:10.760 --> 00:31:14.120
But we went from the
prototype that I built in R

00:31:14.120 --> 00:31:17.600
to having a subcontractor
redoing it in basically a click

00:31:17.600 --> 00:31:18.830
dashboard.

00:31:18.830 --> 00:31:23.000
Which, by the way, I feel is
not as clean and as beautiful

00:31:23.000 --> 00:31:25.040
as some of the leaflet packages.

00:31:25.040 --> 00:31:26.600
That's just my personal opinion.

00:31:26.600 --> 00:31:28.640
But it gets the job done.

00:31:28.640 --> 00:31:32.210
But anyway, I really want to
take that prototype of the map

00:31:32.210 --> 00:31:34.160
that I built and make
it public-facing.

00:31:34.160 --> 00:31:36.290
But there is no way.

00:31:36.290 --> 00:31:38.060
People will come
slamming on my door

00:31:38.060 --> 00:31:40.350
if I have positives
and negatives on a map.

00:31:40.350 --> 00:31:44.240
So my thought was,
instead, to do something

00:31:44.240 --> 00:31:46.670
like here are where
commodities are located,

00:31:46.670 --> 00:31:49.070
because that's not
necessarily sensitive.

00:31:49.070 --> 00:31:51.815
And I would have to
take a public data set.

00:31:51.815 --> 00:31:53.690
Those of you who are
asking about public data

00:31:53.690 --> 00:31:55.830
sets, there are a ton
out there, by the way.

00:31:55.830 --> 00:31:58.740
It's just a matter of finding
a good one for your use case.

00:31:58.740 --> 00:32:01.700
You can literally just
Google Public data sets.

00:32:01.700 --> 00:32:04.470
Academia has their
hands on tons of them.

00:32:04.470 --> 00:32:05.870
So they're definitely out there.

00:32:05.870 --> 00:32:08.360
But I've taken things like--

00:32:08.360 --> 00:32:10.220
feel free to laugh at this--

00:32:10.220 --> 00:32:12.530
I know that, because I
used to work with the Water

00:32:12.530 --> 00:32:18.410
Board of California, that
they have testing for streams,

00:32:18.410 --> 00:32:21.230
like certain fish or
bioassays that are done there.

00:32:21.230 --> 00:32:24.170
And I've taken those
and just jig them,

00:32:24.170 --> 00:32:29.180
move them a fraction of
a GPS point up or down,

00:32:29.180 --> 00:32:30.900
and just been like,
here's my new point.

00:32:30.900 --> 00:32:34.430
And we're going to fake that
here's where lettuce is grown

00:32:34.430 --> 00:32:35.570
or radishes are grown.

00:32:35.570 --> 00:32:39.110
So you might see a radish grown
in the middle of a stream.

00:32:39.110 --> 00:32:42.200
But, hey, I just put a
giant caveat along the top

00:32:42.200 --> 00:32:44.870
that this is meant for
demonstration purposes.

00:32:44.870 --> 00:32:46.550
It's fake data, et cetera.

00:32:46.550 --> 00:32:48.770
But I'm basically going
through that whole process

00:32:48.770 --> 00:32:50.510
for everything that
I've ever built,

00:32:50.510 --> 00:32:52.790
which is not a huge
portfolio, because I

00:32:52.790 --> 00:32:54.740
feel like the most of
the things I've built

00:32:54.740 --> 00:32:58.370
are pretty action-packed
in a single tool.

00:32:58.370 --> 00:33:00.980
So there's a lot of hours
packed into a single tool.

00:33:00.980 --> 00:33:03.020
But I hope that
makes sense on some

00:33:03.020 --> 00:33:05.940
of the ways you can
de-identify some of your data.

00:33:05.940 --> 00:33:09.980
I think it's more important
to show the visualizations

00:33:09.980 --> 00:33:13.850
and to also have a page that
chronicles the process a bit.

00:33:13.850 --> 00:33:16.950
So they understand all the work
that goes in rather than just,

00:33:16.950 --> 00:33:18.463
here's your snapshot.

00:33:18.463 --> 00:33:19.880
Because we do live
in an age where

00:33:19.880 --> 00:33:21.740
people are a little
underwhelmed now,

00:33:21.740 --> 00:33:25.248
thank you CGI and great movies,
by beautiful visualizations.

00:33:25.248 --> 00:33:27.540
So you have to let them know,
there are a lot of blood,

00:33:27.540 --> 00:33:29.360
sweat, and tears
that went into this.

00:33:29.360 --> 00:33:32.330
So anyway, there's
my two-second spiel

00:33:32.330 --> 00:33:36.290
on how to maybe de-identify
some of your work.

00:33:36.290 --> 00:33:37.880
Thank you, Marlene.

00:33:37.880 --> 00:33:39.950
Yeah, that's such
a great point too.

00:33:39.950 --> 00:33:43.970
Because I was even just talking
about that internally at Posit

00:33:43.970 --> 00:33:48.020
that if we show an example
that's using mtcars,

00:33:48.020 --> 00:33:52.190
it's not going to look the
same to a finance company.

00:33:52.190 --> 00:33:53.930
They want to-- even
if it's play data,

00:33:53.930 --> 00:33:56.180
they want to see
something that actually is

00:33:56.180 --> 00:33:58.565
relevant to their industry too.

00:34:01.740 --> 00:34:04.500
Another question for you, Oscar.

00:34:04.500 --> 00:34:08.540
We talk a lot about
this shift from being

00:34:08.540 --> 00:34:11.600
an individual contributor
to moving into management.

00:34:11.600 --> 00:34:13.820
And something I've
seen you write about

00:34:13.820 --> 00:34:18.199
before is hiring to replace you
versus hiring for the skills

00:34:18.199 --> 00:34:21.230
you don't have.

00:34:21.230 --> 00:34:25.400
And just wanted to open up
the discussion about that too.

00:34:25.400 --> 00:34:28.429
Yeah, so this was--

00:34:28.429 --> 00:34:32.510
I'll give credit to, I think
his name's Michael Kaminski

00:34:32.510 --> 00:34:36.685
from the locally
optimistic Slack group.

00:34:36.685 --> 00:34:38.360
It's also highly
recommend for anyone

00:34:38.360 --> 00:34:43.010
who's on this call
to join that as well.

00:34:43.010 --> 00:34:48.730
But, yeah, so it was--

00:34:48.730 --> 00:34:53.719
so when you're getting
into a management position,

00:34:53.719 --> 00:34:55.850
at some point you need
to start hiring people

00:34:55.850 --> 00:34:57.784
and a team around you.

00:34:57.784 --> 00:35:04.140
And hiring is actually
very different

00:35:04.140 --> 00:35:07.080
depending at different
points in your career.

00:35:07.080 --> 00:35:08.730
I thought that it
had been the same,

00:35:08.730 --> 00:35:10.860
because I had done
hiring before,

00:35:10.860 --> 00:35:12.360
even when I wasn't a manager.

00:35:15.000 --> 00:35:18.450
And it does actually come
this split that, I think,

00:35:18.450 --> 00:35:20.550
takes some people by
surprise the first time

00:35:20.550 --> 00:35:23.530
it starts happening, because it
certainly took me by surprise.

00:35:23.530 --> 00:35:28.170
So before you're, let's
say, a manager manager,

00:35:28.170 --> 00:35:30.600
if you are growing
in your career

00:35:30.600 --> 00:35:33.060
and you do happen to
be hiring someone,

00:35:33.060 --> 00:35:36.210
you are hiring someone
most likely to replace you,

00:35:36.210 --> 00:35:38.010
like a more junior
version of yourself.

00:35:38.010 --> 00:35:39.302
And you're going to train them.

00:35:39.302 --> 00:35:42.155
And you are the
expert of some level,

00:35:42.155 --> 00:35:43.530
and you're going
to bring them up

00:35:43.530 --> 00:35:46.440
to your level or
something like that.

00:35:46.440 --> 00:35:50.640
And so, and also you
feel, I suppose, in a way,

00:35:50.640 --> 00:35:52.710
quite secure in
what it is you're

00:35:52.710 --> 00:35:55.590
doing because you've already got
much more knowledge than them.

00:35:55.590 --> 00:35:58.660
And you are bringing
them up to your level.

00:35:58.660 --> 00:36:01.290
And so, you feel very confident
in answering the questions

00:36:01.290 --> 00:36:03.900
and mentoring them
and all of that.

00:36:03.900 --> 00:36:05.910
But it becomes very
different if you're

00:36:05.910 --> 00:36:09.600
starting to hire for
skills you don't have.

00:36:09.600 --> 00:36:13.120
So, for example, I don't
have a stats background.

00:36:13.120 --> 00:36:15.460
I don't have a data
engineering background.

00:36:15.460 --> 00:36:17.070
I don't have even
that much, actually,

00:36:17.070 --> 00:36:18.112
of an analyst background.

00:36:18.112 --> 00:36:22.950
I was a market analyst,
not a data analyst, per se.

00:36:22.950 --> 00:36:27.630
And when you start hiring these
team members into your team,

00:36:27.630 --> 00:36:29.250
these are for
skills I don't have.

00:36:29.250 --> 00:36:32.650
It becomes much more difficult
for me to also assess

00:36:32.650 --> 00:36:34.770
what are they good or not?

00:36:34.770 --> 00:36:38.250
And also, very quickly,
it's kind of weird.

00:36:38.250 --> 00:36:40.590
I first started, like I
think most people start,

00:36:40.590 --> 00:36:43.230
I was trying to learn as much
data engineering and data

00:36:43.230 --> 00:36:45.750
science as I could
to try and just

00:36:45.750 --> 00:36:47.310
stay at least up with them.

00:36:47.310 --> 00:36:49.830
But there's no way
that you can do that.

00:36:49.830 --> 00:36:53.820
And, actually, it's then
the sort of click happens.

00:36:53.820 --> 00:36:55.920
And you're like, OK, wait,
actually, my job is not

00:36:55.920 --> 00:36:59.370
to be the experts of all
of the people in my team.

00:36:59.370 --> 00:37:02.730
I am just the mostly, really,
a glorified facilitator

00:37:02.730 --> 00:37:03.780
of their work.

00:37:03.780 --> 00:37:08.820
And if I know more
than them, that's

00:37:08.820 --> 00:37:10.500
probably actually a problem.

00:37:10.500 --> 00:37:13.770
We can't have me be
with my 10% of time

00:37:13.770 --> 00:37:15.780
on all of these
different vast fields

00:37:15.780 --> 00:37:17.250
be still the expert
in everything.

00:37:17.250 --> 00:37:18.460
That's not going to be great.

00:37:18.460 --> 00:37:20.130
And that's quite a
different switch.

00:37:20.130 --> 00:37:21.605
And when it's--

00:37:21.605 --> 00:37:22.980
I think it takes
a bit of getting

00:37:22.980 --> 00:37:25.770
used to to start
not understanding

00:37:25.770 --> 00:37:27.480
bits of what it is.

00:37:27.480 --> 00:37:29.820
Then the tables
turn a little bit.

00:37:29.820 --> 00:37:32.970
I'm in some ways the
non-technical manager

00:37:32.970 --> 00:37:35.762
of technical people, though
I'm kind of technical myself.

00:37:35.762 --> 00:37:37.470
But they'll talk about
things, and I also

00:37:37.470 --> 00:37:38.637
have to be like, whoa, whoa.

00:37:38.637 --> 00:37:41.430
Then I start also clicking
a lot of what my directors

00:37:41.430 --> 00:37:43.290
and execs feel like sometimes.

00:37:43.290 --> 00:37:46.680
I'm like, yeah, you're getting
into way too much detail.

00:37:46.680 --> 00:37:49.650
I can't do anything with
what you're telling me.

00:37:49.650 --> 00:37:51.960
But then also it
gets much better

00:37:51.960 --> 00:37:54.690
in trying to assess,
OK, what's the options?

00:37:54.690 --> 00:37:57.210
My role mostly is
to try and not just

00:37:57.210 --> 00:38:01.440
prioritize work but
try and help them

00:38:01.440 --> 00:38:05.370
uncover where there's
work needs to be done,

00:38:05.370 --> 00:38:08.610
or if there's a dilemma,
try and break down, well,

00:38:08.610 --> 00:38:10.170
what if we go this direction?

00:38:10.170 --> 00:38:11.760
How bad will it be
if this happens,

00:38:11.760 --> 00:38:13.590
or how good will it
be if this happens?

00:38:13.590 --> 00:38:15.060
Can we weigh risk like this?

00:38:15.060 --> 00:38:18.180
So I can convey to them
as well where things just

00:38:18.180 --> 00:38:19.050
aren't a priority.

00:38:19.050 --> 00:38:21.600
Because no matter what they
do or stress about here,

00:38:21.600 --> 00:38:24.220
it ain't going to matter later.

00:38:24.220 --> 00:38:28.050
So that's quite a
different ballpark.

00:38:28.050 --> 00:38:31.620
And, yeah, I think it probably
happens the first time you hire

00:38:31.620 --> 00:38:33.540
someone that's not a
replacement for you,

00:38:33.540 --> 00:38:36.010
it's an addition to your team.

00:38:36.010 --> 00:38:37.830
So, yeah.

00:38:37.830 --> 00:38:41.000
It can catch you by surprise.

00:38:41.000 --> 00:38:46.040
Any other tips for
us if we're navigating

00:38:46.040 --> 00:38:49.370
that first hire,
where you don't have

00:38:49.370 --> 00:38:51.440
the same skill set as them?

00:38:51.440 --> 00:38:54.560
How you communicate with
them, or in your one-on-ones

00:38:54.560 --> 00:38:55.160
with them?

00:39:00.550 --> 00:39:03.940
I'm very clear that,
up front, that I am not

00:39:03.940 --> 00:39:05.320
the skilled person
that they will

00:39:05.320 --> 00:39:10.252
be able to come to for
x, y, z technical thing.

00:39:10.252 --> 00:39:11.210
I just don't have that.

00:39:11.210 --> 00:39:12.585
And they can't
expect that of me.

00:39:12.585 --> 00:39:17.140
But I am quite clear
about what I can provide

00:39:17.140 --> 00:39:20.800
and what my role is, and
that I am interested, still,

00:39:20.800 --> 00:39:23.410
in their technical
development and in--

00:39:23.410 --> 00:39:27.047
but they need to guide
me about what they think,

00:39:27.047 --> 00:39:28.380
where they think they should go.

00:39:28.380 --> 00:39:31.890
They're going to tell me
some gobbledygook new thing

00:39:31.890 --> 00:39:34.540
like, OK, da, da, da
and then I'm like,

00:39:34.540 --> 00:39:35.730
OK, I just need to assess.

00:39:35.730 --> 00:39:39.090
This is what I'll need to assess
if this is going to be good

00:39:39.090 --> 00:39:40.020
or not.

00:39:40.020 --> 00:39:43.290
So I suppose being much more--

00:39:43.290 --> 00:39:46.440
I'm very clear about
my thought processes

00:39:46.440 --> 00:39:47.950
and my decision-making.

00:39:47.950 --> 00:39:50.070
So I do that very vocally.

00:39:50.070 --> 00:39:53.760
If I'm weighing up things,
I'm talking out loud.

00:39:53.760 --> 00:39:55.410
OK, if we do this, then it's--

00:39:55.410 --> 00:39:57.540
so that it's not this
black box for them

00:39:57.540 --> 00:40:00.150
about they gave me
some information

00:40:00.150 --> 00:40:02.640
and then I just gave
the decision back.

00:40:02.640 --> 00:40:06.240
I think that's also been
quite helpful in getting

00:40:06.240 --> 00:40:12.660
our communication
to work quite well.

00:40:12.660 --> 00:40:14.705
Yeah, especially when
you have with people

00:40:14.705 --> 00:40:16.866
with different backgrounds.

00:40:16.866 --> 00:40:20.790
And I feel like career
development is such a big part

00:40:20.790 --> 00:40:22.450
of being a manager.

00:40:22.450 --> 00:40:24.810
But I don't know if it's
necessarily something

00:40:24.810 --> 00:40:27.990
that managers are always taught.

00:40:27.990 --> 00:40:33.300
And I'm wondering
how do you learn

00:40:33.300 --> 00:40:38.030
about growing your
team's career and having

00:40:38.030 --> 00:40:40.980
those conversations with them?

00:40:40.980 --> 00:40:46.700
Yeah, I think in
one way, now, for many

00:40:46.700 --> 00:40:48.380
this might sound blasphemous.

00:40:48.380 --> 00:40:52.820
But in one way, I try
and take the company out

00:40:52.820 --> 00:40:57.430
of this equation totally,
not just our immediate needs.

00:40:57.430 --> 00:41:00.960
And I think, what's going to be
really great for this person's

00:41:00.960 --> 00:41:02.340
career in general?

00:41:02.340 --> 00:41:06.540
If I bumped into you 5 years
or 10 years down the line,

00:41:06.540 --> 00:41:08.670
in a selfish way, it
would be fantastic

00:41:08.670 --> 00:41:12.030
if your career was
just going swimmingly.

00:41:12.030 --> 00:41:15.930
It's good to have context if
careers are just really flying.

00:41:15.930 --> 00:41:19.500
And then I work
backwards from that

00:41:19.500 --> 00:41:23.452
and then say, OK, I think
would this be better for you?

00:41:23.452 --> 00:41:24.660
Would that be better for you?

00:41:24.660 --> 00:41:26.250
Or don't worry too much.

00:41:26.250 --> 00:41:29.470
If you could do anything,
what would you do?

00:41:29.470 --> 00:41:32.410
And then, of course, you need
to try and whittle down and say,

00:41:32.410 --> 00:41:34.832
could we apply some of that
now in what we're doing?

00:41:34.832 --> 00:41:36.040
Or do you see an opportunity?

00:41:36.040 --> 00:41:38.810
Is it something that
really interests you?

00:41:38.810 --> 00:41:39.810
That really helps a lot.

00:41:39.810 --> 00:41:43.180
But, actually, I found
another way as well

00:41:43.180 --> 00:41:48.320
is to let people go with
their curiosity as well.

00:41:48.320 --> 00:41:51.670
And I think that's one thing
that at least I can appreciate

00:41:51.670 --> 00:41:53.920
coming from some kind
of technical background

00:41:53.920 --> 00:41:56.290
is, actually, most of
my career development

00:41:56.290 --> 00:41:59.620
has been from just the bits
that I was curious about that I

00:41:59.620 --> 00:42:02.410
couldn't explain to
someone exactly what was

00:42:02.410 --> 00:42:04.390
the ROI on this
thing going to be.

00:42:04.390 --> 00:42:06.700
I don't know, it's
just you get a sense

00:42:06.700 --> 00:42:08.770
that some things may pay off.

00:42:08.770 --> 00:42:11.650
Even if it doesn't, you're
going to learn quite a lot.

00:42:11.650 --> 00:42:14.230
So I try and give a
lot of room for if you

00:42:14.230 --> 00:42:17.780
want to go try that, just
go do it type of thing.

00:42:17.780 --> 00:42:22.040
And I trust that some way you'll
benefit and we'll benefit,

00:42:22.040 --> 00:42:23.590
whether it's successful or not.

00:42:23.590 --> 00:42:26.170
I try and make a lot
of room for that.

00:42:26.170 --> 00:42:27.070
Thank you.

00:42:27.070 --> 00:42:27.903
OSCAR BARUFFA: Yeah.

00:42:27.903 --> 00:42:29.528
I want to make sure
I go over to some

00:42:29.528 --> 00:42:30.940
of these anonymous
questions too.

00:42:30.940 --> 00:42:34.030
And somebody had asked,
I have acquired skills

00:42:34.030 --> 00:42:37.120
in project management
and program evaluation.

00:42:37.120 --> 00:42:40.150
Could you elaborate more on
the application of project

00:42:40.150 --> 00:42:42.250
management to data analytics?

00:42:45.500 --> 00:42:46.310
Well, yeah.

00:42:49.080 --> 00:42:54.490
As data is becoming used in
organizations a lot more,

00:42:54.490 --> 00:42:59.430
and if you come from the
digitally-native organization,

00:42:59.430 --> 00:43:01.590
you might not realize this.

00:43:01.590 --> 00:43:05.070
But coming from any kind
of traditional organization

00:43:05.070 --> 00:43:07.680
that's everyone, let's
say, not a digital producer

00:43:07.680 --> 00:43:12.300
of some kind, data is becoming
more and more infused daily

00:43:12.300 --> 00:43:13.020
into work.

00:43:13.020 --> 00:43:15.960
Sometimes not always at the
pace we like, but it is.

00:43:15.960 --> 00:43:21.960
And data as products are
becoming more ubiquitous.

00:43:21.960 --> 00:43:24.060
So someone wants
to develop a tool

00:43:24.060 --> 00:43:26.190
that assesses something,
or a shiny dashboard,

00:43:26.190 --> 00:43:28.410
or any other dashboard,
or data starts

00:43:28.410 --> 00:43:30.270
moving back and
forth between places,

00:43:30.270 --> 00:43:32.370
and these things
need to be managed,

00:43:32.370 --> 00:43:34.710
whether in whole
or just in chunks.

00:43:34.710 --> 00:43:38.460
And I think that's where
the project management

00:43:38.460 --> 00:43:40.200
application from
a data perspective

00:43:40.200 --> 00:43:45.690
comes and will keep
growing as companies

00:43:45.690 --> 00:43:49.740
start adopting that kind of
data as a product mindset.

00:43:49.740 --> 00:43:53.280
Like, oh, this is not
just this inert data.

00:43:53.280 --> 00:43:56.250
It's not just, yeah,
this inert pile of stuff

00:43:56.250 --> 00:43:58.860
that we occasionally will
pick from when we need it.

00:43:58.860 --> 00:44:02.490
It becomes this more living
integral part of work

00:44:02.490 --> 00:44:04.200
that someone needs
to maintain, someone

00:44:04.200 --> 00:44:05.940
needs to have a vision for.

00:44:05.940 --> 00:44:08.220
And then, naturally,
projects around

00:44:08.220 --> 00:44:10.830
that grow because you
want to upgrade things,

00:44:10.830 --> 00:44:14.250
you want to add
new capabilities.

00:44:14.250 --> 00:44:16.260
So that's where
I see it hitting.

00:44:16.260 --> 00:44:20.340
And that's partly also
why this data project

00:44:20.340 --> 00:44:22.470
management for data
analyst book is useful,

00:44:22.470 --> 00:44:25.650
because a lot of data people
find themselves suddenly

00:44:25.650 --> 00:44:27.240
managing a project,
like, oh, I've

00:44:27.240 --> 00:44:31.020
got to get the comms people
involved for the, I don't know,

00:44:31.020 --> 00:44:33.747
the fonts on the
frontend of my thing

00:44:33.747 --> 00:44:34.830
that I'm about to publish.

00:44:34.830 --> 00:44:38.850
Or I need to talk to
just more and more people

00:44:38.850 --> 00:44:42.910
as data starts touching more
bits of the organization.

00:44:42.910 --> 00:44:48.170
You've got to align a lot of
people around some deliverable.

00:44:48.170 --> 00:44:49.400
Definitely.

00:44:49.400 --> 00:44:51.170
Thank you.

00:44:51.170 --> 00:44:54.110
I love all these
packages and resources

00:44:54.110 --> 00:44:56.550
that are being shared in
the chat right now too.

00:44:56.550 --> 00:45:00.320
It looks like there's a lot
of great resources on making

00:45:00.320 --> 00:45:04.430
dummy data sets
and synthetic data.

00:45:04.430 --> 00:45:07.720
Another anonymous
question-- actually,

00:45:07.720 --> 00:45:09.880
first, let's jump to
your question, Sal.

00:45:09.880 --> 00:45:12.895
I see you asked a
question in the chat.

00:45:12.895 --> 00:45:13.990
Do you want to jump in?

00:45:19.940 --> 00:45:23.720
And I can ask it if you want
to jump in with more context.

00:45:23.720 --> 00:45:26.630
But the question was,
at a certain level,

00:45:26.630 --> 00:45:30.050
I feel that managers are
told, let your employees be

00:45:30.050 --> 00:45:34.340
autonomous, but simultaneously
managers and chain of command

00:45:34.340 --> 00:45:36.920
have very specific expectations.

00:45:36.920 --> 00:45:39.680
Do you have experience
in balancing autonomy

00:45:39.680 --> 00:45:42.185
and directing towards
a desired result?

00:45:47.230 --> 00:45:50.470
Yeah, I don't think those
things are mutually exclusive.

00:45:53.400 --> 00:45:56.300
I mean, I suppose maybe,
just personally, I've

00:45:56.300 --> 00:45:57.120
been quite lucky.

00:45:57.120 --> 00:45:59.000
I've always had a
lot of autonomy.

00:46:01.570 --> 00:46:05.050
But I think autonomy--

00:46:05.050 --> 00:46:08.000
now, I'm not talking about,
let's say, toxic workplaces

00:46:08.000 --> 00:46:09.750
where people are
micromanaging and there's

00:46:09.750 --> 00:46:10.792
power plays and all that.

00:46:10.792 --> 00:46:15.600
Let's just say, there's
at least some level

00:46:15.600 --> 00:46:19.500
of maybe autonomy
being earned, perhaps,

00:46:19.500 --> 00:46:21.900
let's say in those
situations where you really

00:46:21.900 --> 00:46:24.360
could be given a lot of
autonomy to achieve your goals,

00:46:24.360 --> 00:46:28.110
but it's still not happening.

00:46:28.110 --> 00:46:32.380
It's earned in the
sense that under--

00:46:32.380 --> 00:46:33.730
it's going to sound so cliche--

00:46:33.730 --> 00:46:36.160
but really understanding
what the business is

00:46:36.160 --> 00:46:39.470
trying to achieve really helps.

00:46:39.470 --> 00:46:40.605
Because then most people--

00:46:40.605 --> 00:46:42.980
in my experience-- most people
don't want to micromanage.

00:46:42.980 --> 00:46:45.860
They want to just let
people run with things right

00:46:45.860 --> 00:46:48.090
and deliver results.

00:46:48.090 --> 00:46:52.730
And I think that
kind of autonomy

00:46:52.730 --> 00:46:56.360
not just comes from
understanding the business,

00:46:56.360 --> 00:47:00.940
but I found a lot of it's
about being very empathetic,

00:47:00.940 --> 00:47:03.010
especially about
those higher up who

00:47:03.010 --> 00:47:07.190
are accountable for the
outputs of whatever it is.

00:47:07.190 --> 00:47:09.530
So and what that
means-- what I mean

00:47:09.530 --> 00:47:14.485
by it is, I often put myself
in the shoes of those who

00:47:14.485 --> 00:47:17.110
are saying they want to turn to
me, or they need these results,

00:47:17.110 --> 00:47:17.610
or whatever.

00:47:17.610 --> 00:47:20.666
And I really think,
OK, what are they--

00:47:20.666 --> 00:47:23.690
if I was them, what am
I trying to deal with?

00:47:23.690 --> 00:47:26.190
I've also got, I didn't sleep
the night before, my kids were

00:47:26.190 --> 00:47:28.620
up, and I've got all these
emails that are in here,

00:47:28.620 --> 00:47:29.910
and da, da, da, da, da, da.

00:47:29.910 --> 00:47:32.640
And I'm about to send
an email that says,

00:47:32.640 --> 00:47:35.190
I can't do this without
these resources.

00:47:35.190 --> 00:47:40.860
I'm going to be like,
that's not going to land.

00:47:40.860 --> 00:47:44.100
And that's it's
maybe one small way

00:47:44.100 --> 00:47:49.580
in doing it is when you practice
that kind of empathetic aspect,

00:47:49.580 --> 00:47:52.410
then it's helped me
a lot phrase things.

00:47:52.410 --> 00:47:56.030
Well, what's the most helpful
I can still be in order

00:47:56.030 --> 00:47:59.510
to move things along, even
if it, something went wrong,

00:47:59.510 --> 00:48:02.030
or I-- and then, that's
really helped me a lot.

00:48:02.030 --> 00:48:03.572
Because then I can
say, well, I've

00:48:03.572 --> 00:48:05.030
also done the work
of really trying

00:48:05.030 --> 00:48:08.240
to understand what it is
we're trying to achieve

00:48:08.240 --> 00:48:13.170
and what the trade-offs
are at the higher up we go.

00:48:13.170 --> 00:48:17.210
And then I'll try and
frame things in the same--

00:48:17.210 --> 00:48:19.520
the alternatives
that are available.

00:48:19.520 --> 00:48:22.010
And then, I also show that
I've thought about if we do x,

00:48:22.010 --> 00:48:23.260
we're not going to reach this.

00:48:23.260 --> 00:48:24.722
But it will maybe do y.

00:48:24.722 --> 00:48:26.430
But if we do this,
it's going to do that.

00:48:26.430 --> 00:48:31.340
And I recommend also, always,
say what you recommend.

00:48:31.340 --> 00:48:34.910
The higher up you go, there's
decision-fatigue like crazy.

00:48:34.910 --> 00:48:36.410
It really helps to
say, I've thought

00:48:36.410 --> 00:48:39.730
about all these trade-offs,
and this is what I recommend.

00:48:39.730 --> 00:48:45.040
So I mean, I can't say if this
will definitely always help.

00:48:45.040 --> 00:48:47.260
But it always feels
like the times

00:48:47.260 --> 00:48:51.820
that I haven't really put in the
effort to do that kind of work,

00:48:51.820 --> 00:48:54.730
then things don't often go
as smoothly or as I'd like.

00:48:54.730 --> 00:48:56.920
Then I get the answers
that I don't always--

00:48:56.920 --> 00:49:01.780
I would prefer maybe not to get,
or the response lands badly.

00:49:01.780 --> 00:49:02.280
Yeah.

00:49:02.280 --> 00:49:03.980
Yeah.

00:49:03.980 --> 00:49:09.870
I wanted to put a plug out
here for your website as well.

00:49:09.870 --> 00:49:12.560
Because I came across
an article that you

00:49:12.560 --> 00:49:15.500
wrote that I found very
interesting on dealing

00:49:15.500 --> 00:49:16.940
with difficult stakeholders.

00:49:16.940 --> 00:49:18.535
Oh, yeah.

00:49:18.535 --> 00:49:19.910
RACHAEL DEMPSEY:
And I would love

00:49:19.910 --> 00:49:23.510
to give some time to that topic.

00:49:23.510 --> 00:49:27.380
And for people who might be
on the call who are hearing,

00:49:27.380 --> 00:49:29.840
we're too busy to go
forward with this project,

00:49:29.840 --> 00:49:32.000
or this is way down
on our priority

00:49:32.000 --> 00:49:36.230
list, what are some things
they can do to get past that?

00:49:36.230 --> 00:49:38.250
Yeah.

00:49:38.250 --> 00:49:48.530
So that post is like a
collection of every, let's say,

00:49:48.530 --> 00:49:51.470
approach that I could
think of that I've

00:49:51.470 --> 00:49:55.760
employed to make working
with stakeholders

00:49:55.760 --> 00:49:57.140
easier in some way.

00:50:00.010 --> 00:50:03.280
And they'll work in
different contexts.

00:50:03.280 --> 00:50:07.420
But I think that for
me, the most effective

00:50:07.420 --> 00:50:08.950
has always been canvassing.

00:50:08.950 --> 00:50:10.367
I think, I don't
know, but I think

00:50:10.367 --> 00:50:13.450
I put eight there or nine, I
can't remember, but canvassing.

00:50:13.450 --> 00:50:17.590
And most of you probably
already do this,

00:50:17.590 --> 00:50:19.977
or you're not sure it's
called canvassing or whatever.

00:50:19.977 --> 00:50:26.280
But when you're getting the
buy-in, especially when there's

00:50:26.280 --> 00:50:27.870
a group, or any
kind of committee,

00:50:27.870 --> 00:50:30.450
or any joint decision,
getting their buy-in

00:50:30.450 --> 00:50:33.930
before the actual
public-facing meeting

00:50:33.930 --> 00:50:37.770
is really the only way to
get really good progress.

00:50:37.770 --> 00:50:41.550
And what's difficult about it
is it's extremely time-consuming

00:50:41.550 --> 00:50:44.220
because, of course, the
bigger the committee is,

00:50:44.220 --> 00:50:47.010
the bigger you've got to go.

00:50:47.010 --> 00:50:52.380
And a key to that is
that trying to understand

00:50:52.380 --> 00:50:56.140
people's incentives, like
politically in the organization

00:50:56.140 --> 00:50:59.200
or whatever, they sense us
for accepting things or not.

00:50:59.200 --> 00:51:02.650
And so, when you're
trying to set up

00:51:02.650 --> 00:51:04.500
these one-on-one
meetings, and that those

00:51:04.500 --> 00:51:06.000
you want to keep quite small.

00:51:06.000 --> 00:51:09.270
Because the best thing
I've found is people

00:51:09.270 --> 00:51:11.550
need to be able to
back out of things.

00:51:11.550 --> 00:51:13.687
If you put people in a
corner and you want their--

00:51:17.040 --> 00:51:19.800
that's what doing publicly in
the group is not a good idea.

00:51:19.800 --> 00:51:21.765
If you're trying
to get people's--

00:51:21.765 --> 00:51:24.000
you want to give
them wiggle room.

00:51:24.000 --> 00:51:28.440
And doing the
one-on-ones beforehand,

00:51:28.440 --> 00:51:31.110
and not following
those up in writing.

00:51:31.110 --> 00:51:32.910
I mean, unless you're
already in some kind

00:51:32.910 --> 00:51:35.535
of combative situation
and you need to do it,

00:51:35.535 --> 00:51:36.420
you need to do it.

00:51:36.420 --> 00:51:38.130
But this is, ideally,
all the legwork

00:51:38.130 --> 00:51:40.607
you do way ahead of
time, that you understand

00:51:40.607 --> 00:51:42.690
where they're coming from,
what they can agree to,

00:51:42.690 --> 00:51:44.040
what they can't agree to.

00:51:44.040 --> 00:51:47.040
And it's great if
you can go around

00:51:47.040 --> 00:51:49.290
and you understand what's
their main concerns

00:51:49.290 --> 00:51:51.900
or what is it they're
looking for from this project

00:51:51.900 --> 00:51:53.220
or from whatever.

00:51:53.220 --> 00:51:55.585
And you build that
in beforehand,

00:51:55.585 --> 00:51:57.210
that ideally you've
got the agreements.

00:51:57.210 --> 00:51:59.252
Then by the time it comes
to the group agreement,

00:51:59.252 --> 00:52:01.210
everyone's already agreed.

00:52:01.210 --> 00:52:04.030
And you will have
to sometimes live

00:52:04.030 --> 00:52:07.180
with this uncertainty of
some people not agreeing,

00:52:07.180 --> 00:52:09.790
but they will at least
not object to the work

00:52:09.790 --> 00:52:12.214
or object to what
it is you're doing.

00:52:12.214 --> 00:52:16.480
And sometimes I
find that was also

00:52:16.480 --> 00:52:20.420
for me, in the first few times
I had to do this kind of thing,

00:52:20.420 --> 00:52:22.390
I wanted the certainty,
so of everyone

00:52:22.390 --> 00:52:25.250
giving me the yes before I
go and spend all this time.

00:52:25.250 --> 00:52:27.610
But the reality is, you
won't get it from everyone.

00:52:27.610 --> 00:52:31.090
But for me, no
objection is as good.

00:52:31.090 --> 00:52:34.630
And usually then after the
thing's got a bit of momentum,

00:52:34.630 --> 00:52:36.550
the objections don't--

00:52:36.550 --> 00:52:38.710
they are too late then later.

00:52:38.710 --> 00:52:41.350
But a lot of people, they
want the ability to back out.

00:52:41.350 --> 00:52:42.850
And sometimes I
think if that's what

00:52:42.850 --> 00:52:46.600
they need to be able to
let this thing go forward,

00:52:46.600 --> 00:52:49.090
then you just got to
be pragmatic about it,

00:52:49.090 --> 00:52:55.020
and then just say, great, that's
as good as it's going to get.

00:52:55.020 --> 00:52:56.610
Thank you.

